r of ranges of value and you know  some of these are not directly relevant  to this for instance the you know the  useful and the harmful there would be a  lot of discussion of that elsewhere even  the noble and the the ignoble or the  face here is primarily interested in the  fact that there is the pleasurable and  the painful and people get mixed up  about this and disagree about them  Aristotle thinks that the person who  actually is virtuous that the person who  has cultivated nobility has the best  understanding of what truly is  pleasurable and what truly is painful  well that means is that we're a bit  mixed up about this and we're liable  until we cultivate virtue to go astray  with respect to evaluating pleasures and  pains it's got another interesting  argument in the 6th point we are prone  from birth to follow pleasure it's a  very natural thing for us Freud actually  calls this the pleasure principle right  that then beatings desire the pleasure  he's not coming up with that by the way  you have materials knew that you know  Plato and Aristotle already knew that so  it's something that we need to be on  guard against and he doesn't say oh we  need to be on guard against it so  therefore pleasure is bad as a matter of  fact pleasures a good thing it's you  know in the range of moral qualities is  a good thing but we do need to be more  careful precisely because it is such  part of our nature the seven point he  says that pleasure and pain are  standards by which we order our actions  the term that he actually uses there in  the Greek is kind of kind of neat kind  of needs of men that's the the first  person plural of it saying that we  regulate or we strictly speaking apply a  manager a cannon a rule so if we're not  careful about how we're applying this  standard we can get ourselves in lots of  trouble if we adopt a very strict  hedonism and say that pleasure is really  the only good that's going to make it  very difficult for us to cultivate  virtue i